Translation patch: Scalar::invert_batch_internal (src/scalar.rs)

Change: Both passes of the batch inversion become index loops:
`for (input, scratch) in inputs.iter_mut().zip(scratch.iter_mut())` becomes `while i < n`
with `inputs[i]` / `scratch[i]`, and `inputs.iter_mut().rev().zip(scratch.iter().rev())`
becomes `k = n; while k > 0 { k -= 1; .. }` with `inputs[k]` / `scratch[k]`.

Why: Aeneas fails on borrows threaded through iterator adaptors over mutable slices
(`iter_mut().rev().zip(..)`): "Internal error" in InterpBorrowsCore.ml.

Behaviour: identical, given `scratch.len() == inputs.len()` (see below). Under that invariant
`zip` pairs index i with i, and the reversed `zip` pairs k with k for k = n-1 .. 0, which is
what the loops visit; the statements run in the same order. `k -= 1` runs only when `k > 0`,
`i += 1` only when `i < n`. Loop bounds depend only on the public length, so constant-time
behaviour is unchanged.

Facts relied on (not visible in the diff): `invert_batch_internal` is private; its only callers are `Scalar::invert_batch`
(`inputs: &mut [Scalar; N]`, `scratch = [one; N]`) and `Scalar::invert_batch_alloc`
(`scratch = vec![one; inputs.len()]`), so `scratch.len() == inputs.len()` always holds
(there is no `debug_assert` of it in this function).

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.
